artifact.self_contained_problem:
Work in the complex projective plane $\mathbb{P}^2 = \mathbb{P}^2_{\mathbb{C}}$ with homogeneous coordinate ring $S = \mathbb{C}[x_0,x_1,x_2]$. Let $C : f = 0$ be a reduced plane curve of degree $d$ with only isolated singularities, where $f \in S_d$. Let $J_f = (\partial_0 f, \partial_1 f, \partial_2 f) \subset S$ be the Jacobian ideal and let $N(f) = I_f / J_f$, where $I_f$ is the saturation of $J_f$ with respect to the maximal ideal $(x_0,x_1,x_2)$. The graded module $N(f) = \bigoplus_j N(f)_j$ is finite dimensional, and one defines the freeness defect
$$\nu(C) \;=\; \max_{j} \dim_{\mathbb{C}} N(f)_j.$$
A reduced curve $C$ is called free if and only if $\nu(C) = 0$.

Question: Is the invariant $\nu(C)$ determined by the following two pieces of data alone:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] the degree $d$ of $C$;
\item[(ii)] the unordered list of analytic isomorphism types of the isolated singularities of $C$ (equivalently, for each singular point $p \in \mathrm{Sing}(C)$, the analytic isomorphism class of the local singularity $(C,p)$)?
\end{enumerate}
That is, do any two reduced plane curves in $\mathbb{P}^2$ with isolated singularities sharing the same degree and the same multiset of analytic singularity types necessarily have the same value of $\nu$?

